Chapter: ClockLocaleTest
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Works chapter GopLocale
  cites Works chapter GopZones

 Stage 1 of `docs/Designs/Active/Apps/ClockAndZone.md`: the calendar and zone
 arithmetic a clock face and a timezone picker are built on. All of it is pure
 over a civil date, which is why all of it is asked here and none of it needs
 a device.

 A DAY OF WEEK THAT IS WRONG IS WRONG ON EVERY DATE AND PLAUSIBLE ON ANY ONE,
 so it is pinned against dates whose answers are known outside this tree
 rather than against its own output. 2000-01-01 was a Saturday; 2024-02-29, a
 leap day, was a Thursday. Both are checked, and the second also says the leap
 rule reached the day-of-week arithmetic rather than only the month length.

 EVERY SUMMER TIME ROW IS A PAIR, one date inside the window and one outside,
 because a rule that answered True always and a rule that answered False
 always each pass half of any single-sided test.

Section: Body

  cl-yn : Boolean -> Text
  cl-yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "NO"

  cl-days : Text
  cl-days =
    "days: 2000-01-01 " & loc-dow-name (loc-dow 2000 1 1)
    & "  is Saturday " & cl-yn (loc-dow 2000 1 1 == 6)
    & "  2024-02-29 " & loc-dow-name (loc-dow 2024 2 29)
    & "  is Thursday " & cl-yn (loc-dow 2024 2 29 == 4)
    & "  1900 leap " & cl-yn (loc-leap 1900)
    & "  2000 leap " & cl-yn (loc-leap 2000)
    & "  Feb 2024 " & show (loc-month-len 2024 2)

 THE LAST SUNDAY IS NOT THE FIFTH. A month with four Sundays has no fifth, so
 a "last" written as an nth would fall off the end of most months. The row
 asks a March with five Sundays and one with four.

  cl-nth : Text
  cl-nth =
    "nth: last Sun Mar 2026 " & show (loc-last-dow 2026 3 0)
    & "  2nd Sun Mar 2026 " & show (loc-nth-dow 2026 3 0 2)
    & "  1st Sun Nov 2026 " & show (loc-nth-dow 2026 11 0 1)
    & "  last Sun Oct 2026 " & show (loc-last-dow 2026 10 0)
    & "  last is in the month " & cl-yn (loc-last-dow 2026 3 0 <= loc-month-len 2026 3)
    & "  and is a Sunday " & cl-yn (loc-dow 2026 3 (loc-last-dow 2026 3 0) == 0)

 EUROPE AND NORTH AMERICA DIFFER BY THREE WEEKS IN SPRING AND ONE IN AUTUMN,
 which is the discrimination that says the two rules are two rules: a single
 shared rule would make these four dates two.

  cl-eu : Text
  cl-eu =
    "EU 2026: starts Mar " & show (loc-last-dow 2026 3 0) & "  ends Oct " & show (loc-last-dow 2026 10 0)
    & "  Jan off " & cl-yn (loc-dst-on loc-dst-eu 2026 1 15 12 == False)
    & "  Jul on " & cl-yn (loc-dst-on loc-dst-eu 2026 7 15 12)
    & "  Dec off " & cl-yn (loc-dst-on loc-dst-eu 2026 12 15 12 == False)

  cl-us : Text
  cl-us =
    "US 2026: starts Mar " & show (loc-nth-dow 2026 3 0 2) & "  ends Nov " & show (loc-nth-dow 2026 11 0 1)
    & "  Jan off " & cl-yn (loc-dst-on loc-dst-us 2026 1 15 12 == False)
    & "  Jul on " & cl-yn (loc-dst-on loc-dst-us 2026 7 15 12)
    & "  earlier than the EU in spring " & cl-yn (loc-nth-dow 2026 3 0 2 < loc-last-dow 2026 3 0)

 THE SOUTHERN WINDOW WRAPS THE YEAR END AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE ROW. Its
 start is a HIGHER month-day than its end, so the plain `start <= now < end`
 test is false for every day of the year and would report a hemisphere that
 never has summer time at all. January is inside the window and July is
 outside it, which is the opposite of every row above.

  cl-south : Text
  cl-south =
    "southern: start " & show (loc-dst-start loc-dst-au 2026) & " end " & show (loc-dst-end loc-dst-au 2026)
    & "  start after end " & cl-yn (loc-dst-start loc-dst-au 2026 > loc-dst-end loc-dst-au 2026)
    & "  Jan on " & cl-yn (loc-dst-on loc-dst-au 2026 1 15 12)
    & "  Jul off " & cl-yn (loc-dst-on loc-dst-au 2026 7 15 12 == False)
    & "  Dec on " & cl-yn (loc-dst-on loc-dst-au 2026 12 15 12)

 THE CHANGEOVER DAY IS DECIDED BY THE HOUR AND ONLY THERE. Both sides of both
 boundaries are asked on the same date, so a rule that ignored the hour would
 give the same answer twice in a row that expects two.

  cl-edge : Text
  cl-edge =
    let s = loc-last-dow 2026 3 0
    in let e = loc-last-dow 2026 10 0
    in "edges: Mar " & show s & " 01h " & cl-yn (loc-dst-on loc-dst-eu 2026 3 s 1)
       & "  03h " & cl-yn (loc-dst-on loc-dst-eu 2026 3 s 3)
       & "  Oct " & show e & " 01h " & cl-yn (loc-dst-on loc-dst-eu 2026 10 e 1)
       & "  03h " & cl-yn (loc-dst-on loc-dst-eu 2026 10 e 3)

 MIDNIGHT AND NOON ARE THE TWO A TWELVE HOUR CLOCK GETS WRONG, so they are
 the two it is asked. Hour 0 must print 12 AM and hour 12 must print 12 PM,
 and a naive `h mod 12` gives 00 for both.

  cl-clock : Text
  cl-clock =
    "clock: 00:05 " & loc-time loc-clock-12 0 5 3 & " / " & loc-time loc-clock-24 0 5 3
    & "   12:05 " & loc-time loc-clock-12 12 5 3 & " / " & loc-time loc-clock-24 12 5 3
    & "   13:05 " & loc-time loc-clock-12 13 5 3
    & "  midnight is twelve " & cl-yn (loc-hour-12 0 == 12)
    & "  noon is twelve " & cl-yn (loc-hour-12 12 == 12)

  cl-date : Text
  cl-date =
    "date 2026-03-09: dmy " & loc-date loc-date-dmy 2026 3 9
    & "  mdy " & loc-date loc-date-mdy 2026 3 9
    & "  ymd " & loc-date loc-date-ymd 2026 3 9
    & "  the three differ " & cl-yn (loc-date loc-date-dmy 2026 3 9 /= loc-date loc-date-mdy 2026 3 9)

 A NEGATIVE OFFSET WITH MINUTES IS WHERE A SIGN GOES WRONG. Newfoundland is
 -03:30; taking the sign after splitting gives "-03:-30".

  cl-off : Text
  cl-off =
    "offsets: " & loc-utc-text 0 & "  " & loc-utc-text 330 & "  " & loc-utc-text (0 - 210)
    & "  " & loc-utc-text 765 & "  " & loc-utc-text (0 - 720)

 ZONES ARE NAMED AND NEVER NUMBERED HERE. An id is the row's position, so
 inserting one row shifts every id after it; this file pinned London, Sydney
 and Kolkata by number, a row was added at the western end, and afterwards it
 measured three other places while still reporting three of its four verdicts
 as passes. `zone-find` is the only way source should reach a particular zone.

  cl-london : Integer = zone-find "Europe/London"
  cl-sydney : Integer = zone-find "Australia/Sydney"
  cl-kolkata : Integer = zone-find "Asia/Kolkata"

 The table is asked through the same door the pane will use, on both sides of
 a transition, so the row proves the rule reached the ZONE and not only the
 calendar. Sydney and London move in opposite directions across the same two
 dates, which no single-hemisphere table could produce.

  cl-zone : Text
  cl-zone =
    "zones: " & show zone-count & " rows"
    & "  found by name " & cl-yn (cl-london > 0 & cl-sydney > 0 & cl-kolkata > 0)
    & "  London Jan " & loc-utc-text (zone-offset-on cl-london 2026 1 15 12)
    & "  Jul " & loc-utc-text (zone-offset-on cl-london 2026 7 15 12)
    & "  Sydney Jan " & loc-utc-text (zone-offset-on cl-sydney 2026 1 15 12)
    & "  Jul " & loc-utc-text (zone-offset-on cl-sydney 2026 7 15 12)
    & "  opposite " & cl-yn (zone-offset-on cl-london 2026 7 15 12 > zone-offset-on cl-london 2026 1 15 12 & zone-offset-on cl-sydney 2026 7 15 12 < zone-offset-on cl-sydney 2026 1 15 12)

 A ZONE THAT NEVER MOVES MUST NEVER MOVE, and Kolkata is the row that says
 both halves at once: a half-hour offset that survives the formatter, and a
 rule of none that survives the date.

  cl-fixed : Text
  cl-fixed =
    "fixed: Kolkata Jan " & loc-utc-text (zone-offset-on cl-kolkata 2026 1 15 12)
    & "  Jul " & loc-utc-text (zone-offset-on cl-kolkata 2026 7 15 12)
    & "  unmoved " & cl-yn (zone-offset-on cl-kolkata 2026 1 15 12 == zone-offset-on cl-kolkata 2026 7 15 12)
    & "  and is half past " & cl-yn (int-mod (zone-offset-on cl-kolkata 2026 7 15 12) 60 == 30)

 ID ZERO IS NOT A ZONE. A machine carrying only the old whole-hour record has
 no zone id, and a zero that read as the first row would put it in the Pacific.

  cl-ids : Text
  cl-ids =
    "ids: zero valid " & cl-yn (zone-valid 0)
    & "  one valid " & cl-yn (zone-valid 1)
    & "  count valid " & cl-yn (zone-valid zone-count)
    & "  past the end valid " & cl-yn (zone-valid (zone-count + 1))
    & "  zero offsets to " & show (zone-offset-on 0 2026 7 15 12)

 EVERY STRIPE THE MAP CAN SHOW IS ASKED FOR ITS CONTENT, because a stripe with
 no zones is a band somebody can click that does nothing, and that is a fact
 about the table worth seeing rather than discovering.

  cl-stripe-line : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  cl-stripe-line (s) (hi) (acc) =
    if s > hi then acc
    else cl-stripe-line (s + 1) hi (acc & (if zone-stripe-count s == 0 then "." else show (zone-stripe-count s)))

  cl-stripe-empty : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cl-stripe-empty (s) (hi) (acc) =
    if s > hi then acc
    else cl-stripe-empty (s + 1) hi (acc + (if zone-stripe-count s == 0 then 1 else 0))

  cl-stripes : Text
  cl-stripes =
    "stripes -12..+14: " & cl-stripe-line zone-stripe-min zone-stripe-max ""
    & "  none empty " & cl-yn (cl-stripe-empty zone-stripe-min zone-stripe-max 0 == 0)
    & "  UTC band first is " & (zone-row-of (zone-stripe-first 0)).zr-city

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    act
      print-line-uni cl-days
      print-line-uni cl-nth
      print-line-uni cl-eu
      print-line-uni cl-us
      print-line-uni cl-south
      print-line-uni cl-edge
      print-line-uni cl-clock
      print-line-uni cl-date
      print-line-uni cl-off
      print-line-uni cl-zone
      print-line-uni cl-fixed
      print-line-uni cl-ids
      print-line-uni cl-stripes
      print-line-uni "clock-locale: reached the end"
    end
